// The Windows resource that gives an executable its icon, and nothing else.
//
// It lives beside the artwork rather than beside a target because two targets
// carry it -- the host, and the platform tests, which are the only thing that
// can prove the resource can be read back out of a binary.
//
// **This is the artifact's identity, not a file beside it** (roadmap M8). A PNG
// next to the binary survives until the first install, move or pack; a resource
// travels with the executable wherever it goes, and it is what Explorer, the
// taskbar and the Alt-Tab switcher read.
//
// The icon is `branding/icon/luaug.ico`, which carries every size from 16 to
// 256 in one resource -- the OS picks per context, and a single 256 downscaled
// by the shell is what makes an icon look cheap in a title bar.
//
// **It is the engine's mark and it is the FALLBACK.** A game built with
// `luaug build` has this resource replaced with its own, from `[project] icon`
// in its `luaug.toml`: an engine whose games all wear the engine's face is a
// template, not an engine. `tools/iconpatch` does the replacing and can read
// the result back out again, which is what makes the claim checkable.
//
// Ordinal 1 rather than a name, because that is what `luaug build` looks for and
// what the shell picks when it wants "the application's icon".
1 ICON "icon/luaug.ico"
